//
// Expected: a0a1 at one session, a1a1 at two — SPLIT is a definition_check
// primitive, so it may carry `?`, and a SPLIT whose argument does not reduce
// to a CONCAT is a FAILED CHECK rather than merely a stuck term. The two
// flights are identical but for that one character.
//
// Under the check, every ciphertext the attacker can build decrypts to
// something that is not a bundle, the projection fails, and Bob is truncated
// before the field is used. Without it Bob carries on holding an irreducible
// SPLIT, uses its first projection anyway, and the authentication query sees a
// value the attacker chose.
//
// The two flights are keyed separately, which is load-bearing: under one key
// the attacker would swap the two ciphertexts, the checked SPLIT would succeed
// on a genuine bundle, and both queries would fail for a reason that has
// nothing to do with checking.
//
// At two sessions the checked flight fails anyway, for a reason that has
// nothing to do with checking: lo, hi and both keys are knows constants, so
// each bundle is the same in every session. One copy of Alice never starts,
// the attacker replays the other's genuine bundle_checked to both of Bob's
// runs, the checked SPLIT succeeds on it, and Bob accepts twice what Alice
// sent once. The executor once ran every copy of every principal to
// completion, so each Bob run had an Alice emission to match. The checking
// contrast is the one-session code.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k_checked, k_loose
	knows private lo, hi
	bundle_checked = ENC(k_checked, CONCAT(lo, hi))
	bundle_loose = ENC(k_loose, CONCAT(hi, lo))
]

Alice -> Bob: bundle_checked, bundle_loose

principal Bob[
	knows private k_checked, k_loose
	x_checked, y_checked = SPLIT(DEC(k_checked, bundle_checked))?
	x_loose, y_loose = SPLIT(DEC(k_loose, bundle_loose))
	_ = HASH(x_checked, x_loose)
]

queries[
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: bundle_checked
	authentication? Alice -> Bob: bundle_loose
]
